TEST DIFX using "Synthetic" data

In this directory is a script to use "generateVDIF" do create fake VDIF data
with a variety of differenr properties (real, complex, USB, LSB etc). Then the
files can be cross correlated to check DIFX is properly handling the different
setups. The "location" of the antenna is quite close together to result in a
small (but non-zero) fringe rate. This will result in a slowly varying phase.

createData.sh will generate the data. By default it will use 20min, but this
can be reduced to speed up generation time. Do not increase beyond 1200 sec.

run-all.sh will run DIFX 7 times for a bunch of different combinations and
generate an IDI-FITS file. No attempt is made to compare the correlated data.

The specfic tests are:

run-usb.sh			2 real USB real sets
run-lsb.sh			2 real, USB vs LSB
run-usb-complex.sh		Real USB vs complex single sideband (USB)
run-lsb-complex.sh		Real USB vs complex single sideband (LSB)
run-usb-dsb.sh			Real USB vs complex double sideband (USB)
run-lsb-dsb.sh			Real USB vs complex double sideband (LSB)
run-complex-complex.sh		Complex USB vs Complex USB (single sindeband)

Two further scenarios exist for the CPU-vs-GPU harnesses (run-local.sh,
run-slurm.sh) rather than run-all.sh:

test-multi			5 stations x 4 subbands - the multi-datastream
				and multi-band machinery
test-dualpol			2 stations, 2 polarisations at the SAME sky
				frequency, doPolar = True. The only scenario with
				more than 2 pol products, so the ONLY one that
				exercises the cross-polarisation autocorrelations
				(on either the CPU or the GPU path). Added
				2026-08-28; sized to run as committed (nChan=1024,
				tInt=2, 60 s scan) rather than needing the manual
				scale-down the older scenarios get.


AUTOMATED REGRESSION ON A SLURM CLUSTER (run-slurm.sh)
------------------------------------------------------

run-all.sh just correlates the data and makes no attempt to check that the
result is correct. run-slurm.sh instead turns these same scenarios into a
pass/fail regression suite for use on a SLURM cluster (e.g. OzSTAR). For each
scenario it correlates the data twice - once on CPU and once on GPU (mpifxcorr
--usegpu) - as separate batch jobs, then uses diffDiFX.py to compare the raw
visibility output and reports PASS/FAIL/ERROR. This is intended as the safety
net when changing the GPU correlator: CPU and GPU output should agree.

Setup (once):

    cp slurm.conf.example slurm.conf
    # edit slurm.conf: account, setup script, working directory, per-job
    # SLURM resources, comparison threshold (see comments in that file)

Run:

    ./run-slurm.sh            # all scenarios
    ./run-slurm.sh usb lsb    # just the named scenarios

It creates one self-contained job directory per scenario and mode under WORKDIR
(from slurm.conf), generates the .input/.calc/.im there with vex2difx+difxcalc,
submits the batch jobs, and once they finish diffs the outputs. A final summary
table shows one row per scenario:

    PASS  - CPU and GPU visibilities agree to within DIFF_THRESHOLD
    FAIL  - a difference exceeded the threshold, or the headers disagreed
    ERROR - a correlation job failed or produced no output to compare

run-slurm.sh exits non-zero if any comparison is not PASS. Detailed per-file
diff logs are left under WORKDIR/<scenario>/diff/.

Optional reference-version comparison: set REFERENCE_SETUP_SCRIPT in slurm.conf
to a setup script for a trusted reference DiFX build (e.g. a released DiFX-2.9).
When set, each scenario is also correlated with that build (CPU only) and the
current CPU and GPU outputs are additionally diffed against it, adding
cpu:reference and gpu:reference columns to the summary. This catches drift
against a known-good baseline, not just CPU-vs-GPU disagreement within the
branch under test. Leave it blank (the default) to skip the reference leg.

The existing run-*.sh / run-all.sh scripts are unchanged and still work for
local (mpirun, no correctness check) runs. slurm.conf and all generated test
artifacts are git-ignored.


